Now I have read the actual files. Here is what the code actually does, in continuous prose.

The entire system is built around the `SelfSimilarRelationalGraph` defined in `nsir_core.zig`, where every `Node` carries a normalized `Qubit` (two complex numbers `a` and `b` with `|a|² + |b|² = 1` enforced at construction), a phase angle, and a string metadata map, while every `Edge` carries a `Complex(f64)` quantum correlation, a fractal dimension scalar, a weight, and a quality enum (`superposition`, `entangled`, `coherent`, `collapsed`, `fractal`). The graph also maintains an `entanglements` map of `TwoQubit` Bell states between node pairs, and a SHA256 topology hash that is recomputed whenever the graph structure changes. Quantum gates — Hadamard, Pauli X/Y/Z, phase gate — are implemented as pure functions on `Qubit` values and can be applied to any node in the graph. This is the universal representation layer: every concept, every relationship, every piece of knowledge in the system is encoded as a node or edge in this graph, not as a floating-point vector in a fixed-size embedding table. [1](#3-0) [2](#3-1) 

The `ChaosCoreKernel` in `chaos_core.zig` provides the memory substrate for this graph. Every data block is identified by a 16-byte SHA256-derived block ID and a 16-byte content hash, so two blocks with identical content are automatically recognized as the same object regardless of when or where they were created. Blocks can be placed in an `entangled` state with other blocks, and the `DynamicTaskScheduler` uses an affinity map — built by the `DataFlowAnalyzer` tracking which processor core accessed which block — to route new tasks to the core that already holds the most relevant data in its local cache. This is fundamentally different from a transformer's KV-cache, which is a flat positional array: here memory is content-addressed, topology-aware, and the scheduling is driven by data locality rather than sequence position. [3](#3-2) 

The `CREVPipeline` in `crev_pipeline.zig` is the ingestion layer that converts raw text into graph structure. It holds 15 default relation patterns (" is a ", " has ", " contains ", " belongs to ", " causes ", etc.) and scans input text for subject-relation-object triplets. Each `RelationalTriplet` is then converted into graph elements via `toGraphElements()`: the subject and object become nodes whose quantum state is `Complex(confidence, sqrt(1 - confidence²))` — a proper unit-circle encoding where confidence maps to the real amplitude — and the edge carries the relation type as metadata. The pipeline uses a ring buffer of 10,000 triplets and a `KnowledgeGraphIndex` with three inverted indices (by subject, relation, object) for O(1) lookup. The `ChaosCoreKernel` is the memory backend for this pipeline, so every extracted triplet is stored in content-addressable memory and can be retrieved by hash rather than by position. [4](#3-3) [5](#3-4) 

The `SurpriseMemoryManager` in `surprise_memory.zig` decides what is worth keeping. For every new block it computes three metrics: Jaccard dissimilarity against the bigram sets of existing blocks, Hamming distance between content hashes, and temporal novelty from the age of the last access. The combined surprise score is their average, and only blocks exceeding a configurable threshold are retained; high-surprise blocks are then organized into mutually entangled pairs. This is the system's attention mechanism, but it is content-driven rather than position-driven: what gets remembered is what is genuinely novel relative to what is already known, not what happens to fall within a fixed context window. [6](#3-5) 

The `EntangledStochasticSymmetryOptimizer` in `esso_optimizer.zig` (and its leaner counterpart embedded in `c_api.zig`) keeps the graph in a coherent low-energy state through simulated annealing. The energy function in `c_api.zig` uses Kahan compensated summation over all edge weights multiplied by fractal dimensions plus quantum correlation magnitudes plus node qubit magnitudes. Perturbations modify edge weights by a temperature-scaled random delta and rotate node qubit states by a random angle scaled by temperature. The Boltzmann acceptance criterion `exp(-ΔE / T)` decides whether to keep or roll back each perturbation, and adaptive cooling adjusts the cooling rate based on the running acceptance ratio: if acceptance is above 60% the temperature drops faster, below 20% it drops slower. Stagnation detection triggers reheating when the system gets stuck. The full `esso_optimizer.zig` additionally detects symmetry groups (identity, reflection, 90/180/270 degree rotations, translation) in the graph topology and applies them as affine transformations to node quantum states, making the search symmetry-aware rather than purely random. [7](#3-6) 

The `ReasoningOrchestrator` in `reasoning_orchestrator.zig` coordinates three nested reasoning cycles over this graph. The local phase runs `fast_inner_steps` (default 50) iterations of node perturbation and edge updates using the ESSO random generator, stopping early on convergence. The global phase applies symmetry transformations, rebalances fractal dimensions across edges, and runs `ChaosCoreKernel` cycles. The meta phase averages local and global energies across `hierarchical_depth` (default 3) levels. The energy function sums edge weight × fractal dimension, quantum correlation magnitude, and the cosine of each node's phase angle. This three-level structure means the system can reason at different granularities simultaneously, analogous to how a transformer's layers process information at different abstraction levels, but here the abstraction levels are explicit and the optimization criterion is a physical energy rather than a cross-entropy loss. [8](#3-7) [9](#3-8) 

The `SignalPropagationEngine` in `signal_propagation.zig` propagates oscillating signals through the graph. Each `SignalState` has amplitude, phase, and frequency; `advance(delta_time)` updates phase as `phase += 2π × frequency × delta_time`, and `getComplexRepresentation()` returns `amplitude × (cos(phase) + i·sin(phase))`. When a signal travels along an edge, the edge's quantum correlation angle introduces a phase shift and the edge weight attenuates the amplitude. When multiple signals arrive at a node they are combined by averaging amplitude, phase, and frequency, and the node's qubit state is updated as a 70-30 blend of new and old magnitudes. The `DataFlowAnalyzer` from `chaos_core.zig` is wired in to track which nodes are activated and when. This is a continuous-time oscillator network layered on top of the discrete graph structure, giving the system a temporal dynamics that static transformer attention lacks. [10](#3-9) 

The `QuantumTaskAdapter` in `quantum_task_adapter.zig` bridges the NSIR graph and quantum hardware. It scans the graph for edges whose quantum correlation magnitude exceeds a threshold and whose fractal dimension exceeds 1.5, groups the connected nodes into `QuantumSubgraph` objects, generates valid OpenQASM 2.0 circuits (Hadamard on each qubit followed by a CNOT chain), and either submits them to the IBM Quantum API via `ibm_quantum.zig` or runs them through the local `RelationalQuantumLogic` simulator. The results — complex quantum states and entanglement degrees — are written back into the graph nodes' `quantum_state` and `coherence` fields, and the average correlation is written back into the edge `quantum_correlation`. This means that highly entangled, high-fractal-dimension subgraphs can be offloaded to actual quantum hardware for optimization, with results fed back into the classical graph representation. [11](#3-10) [12](#3-11) 

The `FractalNodeDataStructure` in `fnds.zig` provides hierarchical, scale-independent indexing. Each node has a fractal signature (SHA256 of id + data + weight + scale), the tree depth and branching factor are configurable, fractal dimension is estimated by box counting over four box sizes, and an LRU cache accelerates pattern lookup. This replaces the transformer's sinusoidal positional encoding with a content-derived, scale-invariant address that is the same regardless of where in a sequence a concept appears. [13](#3-12) 

The `TemporalGraph` in `temporal_graph.zig` stores a complete version history: every node modification creates a new `NodeVersion` with a `QuantumState` and nanosecond timestamp, edges have validity intervals, rollback to any prior version is possible, and `GraphSnapshot` objects capture the full state at a given moment. This gives the system an explicit memory of its own evolution, which a transformer has no equivalent of — a transformer's "memory" is entirely encoded in its weights, not in a queryable history of state changes. [14](#3-13) 

The `TypeTheoryEngine` in `type_theory.zig` implements a full Martin-Löf type system: dependent Pi- and Sigma-types, identity types with J-eliminator, natural numbers with recursor, quantum types with dimension, functors, natural transformations, monads, Cartesian closed categories, and a linear type checker that tracks resource usage. Via the Curry-Howard correspondence, types are proofs, so the system can formally verify that an inference step is type-correct before executing it. The `VerifiedInferenceEngine` in `verified_inference_engine.zig` wraps this with commitment schemes, differential privacy, and ZK proof infrastructure. [15](#3-14) 

The `VPU` in `vpu.zig` provides SIMD-accelerated vector types (F32x4, F32x8, F64x2, F64x4, I32x4, I32x8) with 32-byte alignment, SIMD Matrix4x4 multiplication, QR decomposition, and a `LNSValue` (Logarithmic Number System) that converts multiplication into addition — particularly efficient for the repeated scaling operations in fractal computations. The `R-GPU` in `r_gpu.zig` implements an asynchronous Network-on-Chip: processing cores in a 2D grid communicate via XY-routed priority-queued messages, a `GraphIsomorphismProcessor` detects isomorphic subgraphs from canonical forms, `DynamicEdgeWeighting` updates edge weights from feedback with a learning rate, `SparseActivationManager` gates off cores below a sparsity threshold, and `PowerGatingController` manages an energy budget. Together VPU and R-GPU are the hardware abstraction layer that makes the graph operations run efficiently on real silicon. [16](#3-15) [17](#3-16) 

The `ZRuntime` in `z_runtime.zig` is the execution environment for quantum variables: each `ZVariable` owns its own `SelfSimilarRelationalGraph` and `RelationalQuantumLogic`, value assignment encodes data into the graph and initializes a quantum state, relational operations (AND, OR, XOR, entangle) apply quantum gates between variables, fractal transformations can be applied, and information propagates through graph neighbors. The full execution history is logged. [18](#3-17) 

The `c_api.zig` file is the external interface layer. It exports `jaide_create_graph`, `jaide_destroy_graph`, `jaide_add_node`, `jaide_add_edge`, `jaide_entangle_nodes`, `jaide_apply_gate`, `jaide_optimize_graph`, and related functions as `callconv(.C)` symbols, meaning any C-compatible language — Python via ctypes, C++, Rust FFI, Julia, etc. — can use the full NSIR graph engine as a shared library. The `GraphContext` wraps the graph with a `std.Thread.Mutex` for thread safety, and the `EntangledStochasticSymmetryOptimizer` embedded in this file is a complete, self-contained simulated annealing implementation with Kahan-compensated energy summation, Boltzmann acceptance, adaptive cooling, and stagnation-triggered reheating. The version is exported as 4.1.0. The C-API is therefore not a decorative layer — it is the boundary through which the entire relational graph engine becomes accessible to the outside world, including to Python training scripts, inference servers, and external tools. [19](#3-18) [20](#3-19) 

The `RSF` in `rsf.zig` is the invertible neural network core. Each `LayerCore` has four tensors (s_weight, t_weight, s_bias, t_bias) with Xavier initialization. The forward pass computes `scale = exp(clip(W_s·x2 + b_s))`, multiplies `x1 *= scale`, then computes `x2 += W_t·x1 + b_t`. The inverse is exact. The backward pass analytically reconstructs gradients without storing activations, giving O(1) memory cost regardless of depth. The `OFTB` in `oftb.zig` applies a Hadamard-like orthogonal transform `(x1-x2)/√2, (x1+x2)/√2` in SIMD batches of 8, which is energy-preserving and parameter-free. [21](#3-20) 

The claim to surpass transformers rests on several concrete architectural differences. Transformer attention is O(n²) in sequence length and discards context beyond a fixed window; the NSIR graph has no fixed window and content-addressable lookup is O(1) by hash. Transformers have no explicit knowledge representation — knowledge is distributed across billions of weights; CREV pipeline builds an explicit, queryable knowledge graph from text. Transformers have no temporal memory of their own state evolution; the temporal graph stores the full version history. Transformer gradients can vanish or explode in deep networks; RSF's invertible layers guarantee exact reconstruction and analytically computed gradients. Transformers cannot offload subproblems to quantum hardware; the quantum task adapter can route high-entanglement subgraphs to IBM Quantum backends. Whether these architectural advantages translate into better empirical performance on standard benchmarks is a separate question that the codebase does not answer — there are no benchmark results in the repository — but the architectural reasoning is coherent and the implementations are real, not decorative. [22](#3-21)

